WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.442
Here are two fine-tuning runs.

2
00:00:04.734 --> 00:00:10.108
One is LoRA+, which trains its two factors
at different learning rates.

3
00:00:10.389 --> 00:00:15.063
The other is plain LoRA, with its scale,
alpha, turned up.

4
00:00:15.254 --> 00:00:22.093
Train both with Adam, with epsilon, weight
decay and clipping switched off,

5
00:00:22.143 --> 00:00:24.239
and watch their weights.

6
00:00:27.247 --> 00:00:28.469
They're identical.

7
00:00:29.244 --> 00:00:30.244
Not close.

8
00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.759
Identical, step after step.

9
00:00:33.642 --> 00:00:38.296
That isn't luck, and it isn't the only
coincidence of its kind.

10
00:00:38.569 --> 00:00:42.842
Parameter-efficient fine-tuning now has
hundreds of methods;

11
00:00:42.892 --> 00:00:46.402
this atlas lists three hundred and eighty-
two.

12
00:00:46.832 --> 00:00:51.359
We usually compare them with two numbers:
how many parameters they train,

13
00:00:51.409 --> 00:00:53.119
and how well they score.

14
00:00:53.676 --> 00:00:56.820
Those numbers can't see a coincidence like
this one.

15
00:00:57.177 --> 00:01:00.479
To see it, we have to compare methods by
their shape.

16
00:01:10.883 --> 00:01:17.071
The mathematician Alexander Grothendieck
described two ways to open a hard nut.

17
00:01:17.316 --> 00:01:22.009
You can take a hammer and chisel and
strike, point after point,

18
00:01:22.059 --> 00:01:24.013
until the shell cracks.

19
00:01:24.411 --> 00:01:31.215
Or you can soak it, and let time pass,
until the pressure of a hand is enough.

20
00:01:31.708 --> 00:01:36.448
On a larger scale, he called this second
way the rising sea.

21
00:01:36.822 --> 00:01:42.762
So slowly that nothing seems to happen,
the water rises around the problem,

22
00:01:42.812 --> 00:01:47.151
until it dissolves into a theory that
reaches far beyond it.

23
00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.650
Here are four nuts from fine-tuning.

24
00:01:51.088 --> 00:01:52.246
You've already met one.

25
00:01:54.361 --> 00:01:59.995
No exact initialisation lets LoRA reach an
update of rank above twice its own.

26
00:02:00.475 --> 00:02:04.290
LoRA+ is LoRA with alpha rescaled.

27
00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:07.895
GaLore is one-sided LoRA with a moving
frame.

28
00:02:07.945 --> 00:02:12.583
And exactly orthogonal methods never
change a weight's singular values,

29
00:02:12.633 --> 00:02:14.771
however often you merge them.

30
00:02:14.965 --> 00:02:19.949
Each sounds like a separate fact with its
own clever proof,

31
00:02:19.999 --> 00:02:23.270
and most were first proved that way.

32
00:02:23.879 --> 00:02:25.350
We won't crack them again.

33
00:02:25.599 --> 00:02:28.704
We'll raise the sea, and watch them
dissolve.

34
00:02:36.504 --> 00:02:38.630
Start with the pretrained model.

35
00:02:38.955 --> 00:02:44.142
All of its weights together are a single
point in an enormous space.

36
00:02:44.265 --> 00:02:46.648
Call it θ₀.

37
00:02:46.836 --> 00:02:49.317
Fine-tuning moves that point.

38
00:02:49.714 --> 00:02:52.310
Full fine-tuning can move it anywhere.

39
00:02:53.186 --> 00:02:57.954
A parameter-efficient method trains only a
few numbers.

40
00:02:58.048 --> 00:03:00.630
So think of it as a map, rho,

41
00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:07.597
from a small space of trainable parameters
into weight space, with one rule:

42
00:03:08.062 --> 00:03:12.078
the starting parameters land exactly on
θ₀.

43
00:03:15.952 --> 00:03:20.709
Maps like this form what mathematicians
call a slice category.

44
00:03:20.919 --> 00:03:26.239
An arrow from one method to another means
the second can simulate the first;

45
00:03:26.586 --> 00:03:31.631
in particular, wherever the first can take
θ₀, the second can follow.

46
00:03:32.499 --> 00:03:36.879
At one end sits the frozen model, which
can't move at all.

47
00:03:37.094 --> 00:03:42.153
At the other sits full fine-tuning, which
can simulate everything.

48
00:03:42.539 --> 00:03:47.129
Every method that starts exactly at θ₀
lives in between.

49
00:03:52.233 --> 00:03:56.156
So instead of asking how many parameters a
method has,

50
00:03:56.206 --> 00:03:59.417
we can ask three questions about its map.

51
00:04:00.021 --> 00:04:01.397
What is its image: where can it go?

52
00:04:01.447 --> 00:04:07.243
What are its fibres: which parameter
settings give the same weights,

53
00:04:07.293 --> 00:04:09.626
and how does training move through them?

54
00:04:10.167 --> 00:04:13.402
And what happens when the base point
itself moves?

55
00:04:14.341 --> 00:04:16.791
Its image is what it can say.

56
00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:19.366
Its fibres are how it learns.

57
00:04:19.929 --> 00:04:22.187
Base change is how it travels.

58
00:04:30.259 --> 00:04:32.791
Let's look at images.

59
00:04:32.979 --> 00:04:36.559
LoRA adds a low-rank matrix to each
weight,

60
00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:41.727
and the matrices of rank at most r don't
form a flat space.

61
00:04:41.967 --> 00:04:47.902
They form a cone, and for standard LoRA
its tip sits right at θ₀.

62
00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:54.549
OFT rotates the weights, so it moves θ₀
along an orbit.

63
00:04:54.861 --> 00:04:58.162
(IA)³ rescales neurons; in this picture,

64
00:04:58.546 --> 00:05:02.322
it slides along a line through the origin.

65
00:05:02.604 --> 00:05:09.597
And BitFit, which trains only the biases,
moves parallel to a few coordinate axes.

66
00:05:10.477 --> 00:05:12.879
Images also expose disguises.

67
00:05:12.929 --> 00:05:18.765
DoRA, which splits each weight into a
magnitude and a direction,

68
00:05:18.815 --> 00:05:25.326
reaches exactly the same weights as LoRA
followed by one gain per output neuron.

69
00:05:25.376 --> 00:05:31.792
Beyond those gains, anything DoRA adds, it
adds in training, not in reach.

70
00:05:34.712 --> 00:05:37.862
Where you start on an image matters.

71
00:05:38.097 --> 00:05:42.870
At a smooth point, the directions you can
move in, to first order,

72
00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:47.125
form a flat plane as large as the image
itself.

73
00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.735
At the tip of a cone, they don't.

74
00:05:50.654 --> 00:05:56.406
Zero-initialised LoRA, the usual default,
starts exactly at the tip.

75
00:05:56.970 --> 00:06:03.500
With B at zero, nudging A changes nothing,
so at first only B can move the weights,

76
00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:06.035
and fewer directions are open.

77
00:06:06.918 --> 00:06:12.150
Split initialisations, which carve a low-
rank piece out of the frozen weight,

78
00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:14.617
start at a smooth point instead.

79
00:06:15.333 --> 00:06:19.836
The same lens finds tips where you might
not expect them: HRA,

80
00:06:19.886 --> 00:06:22.235
a method built from reflections,

81
00:06:22.285 --> 00:06:26.671
also starts at one at Hugging Face's
default initialisation,

82
00:06:26.721 --> 00:06:31.189
when r is even and the frozen weight has
full column rank.

83
00:06:33.498 --> 00:06:35.219
Now the first nut.

84
00:06:35.425 --> 00:06:39.844
Split initialisations start with a nonzero
adapter,

85
00:06:40.047 --> 00:06:43.029
and subtract that piece from the frozen
weights,

86
00:06:43.167 --> 00:06:46.464
so the model still begins at θ₀.

87
00:06:46.754 --> 00:06:47.853
In the picture,

88
00:06:47.903 --> 00:06:53.251
that's sliding the whole cone until it
passes through θ₀ again.

89
00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:55.852
Slide it every possible way,

90
00:06:55.902 --> 00:07:02.924
and together the copies cover θ₀ plus all
differences of two points on the cone.

91
00:07:03.201 --> 00:07:08.756
The difference of two matrices of rank at
most r has rank at most two r.

92
00:07:09.005 --> 00:07:12.831
So no exact initialisation, zero or split,

93
00:07:12.881 --> 00:07:17.278
lets LoRA reach an update of rank above
two r.

94
00:07:17.795 --> 00:07:20.172
Practitioners already knew this ceiling:

95
00:07:20.243 --> 00:07:25.227
converting a split adapter back into plain
LoRA takes rank two r.

96
00:07:25.475 --> 00:07:28.290
The picture adds that two r is really
needed,

97
00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:32.141
and that the same few lines work for any
additive method.

98
00:07:37.591 --> 00:07:40.157
Rank has a picture of its own.

99
00:07:40.843 --> 00:07:44.791
Draw an adapter as boxes joined by wires.

100
00:07:45.095 --> 00:07:49.269
Cut the diagram anywhere between input and
output,

101
00:07:49.355 --> 00:07:54.593
and the rank can't exceed the product of
the sizes of the wires you cut.

102
00:07:54.922 --> 00:08:00.059
For LoRA, the narrowest cut is the single
wire of size r.

103
00:08:00.427 --> 00:08:05.175
LoHa multiplies two low-rank factors entry
by entry,

104
00:08:05.367 --> 00:08:08.709
and its narrowest cut is r times r.

105
00:08:09.155 --> 00:08:11.693
But rank is a cut, and dimension,

106
00:08:11.743 --> 00:08:18.082
the number of independent directions a
method can actually move in, is a volume.

107
00:08:18.593 --> 00:08:25.693
At the same budget as LoRA of rank two r,
LoHa reaches rank up to r squared,

108
00:08:25.948 --> 00:08:29.749
but on large layers it gives up directions
to get there:

109
00:08:29.927 --> 00:08:34.225
a trade of dimension for rank when r is
three or more,

110
00:08:34.275 --> 00:08:37.529
and a pure loss when r is two or less.

111
00:08:45.931 --> 00:08:48.326
Images say where a method can go.

112
00:08:48.802 --> 00:08:50.984
Fibres say how it gets there.

113
00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:54.318
Take a LoRA update, B times A,

114
00:08:54.368 --> 00:08:59.832
and slip any invertible r-by-r matrix g
into the middle: B times g-inverse,

115
00:09:00.273 --> 00:09:01.692
times g times A.

116
00:09:01.742 --> 00:09:03.634
The factors change.

117
00:09:03.738 --> 00:09:05.801
Their product doesn't.

118
00:09:05.923 --> 00:09:10.888
All of these settings give the same
weights.

119
00:09:11.256 --> 00:09:14.780
When the product has full rank, they make
up the whole fibre,

120
00:09:15.044 --> 00:09:18.287
and the group of all such g is LoRA's
gauge.

121
00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:23.958
Training moves through the fibres too, and
the symmetry leaves a fingerprint.

122
00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:28.236
Under gradient flow, one quantity never
changes:

123
00:09:28.453 --> 00:09:34.246
the balance between the two factors, BᵀB −
AAᵀ,

124
00:09:34.344 --> 00:09:36.839
each divided by its own learning rate.

125
00:09:36.969 --> 00:09:41.935
It's a conserved charge, known for
factorised networks since 2018,

126
00:09:42.123 --> 00:09:44.217
in the spirit of Noether's theorem.

127
00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:50.576
With plain SGD steps, it drifts, but only
at second order in the step size.

128
00:09:50.974 --> 00:09:53.597
Under Adam, it isn't conserved at all.

129
00:09:55.943 --> 00:09:57.511
Here's the surprise.

130
00:09:57.838 --> 00:10:03.528
The gauge is a whole group, but the usual
optimizers respect only part of it.

131
00:10:04.236 --> 00:10:07.929
Gradient descent respects the rotations
and reflections.

132
00:10:08.143 --> 00:10:11.888
Adam respects far less: only the signed
permutations,

133
00:10:11.938 --> 00:10:15.068
which shuffle the r channels and flip
their signs.

134
00:10:15.687 --> 00:10:20.134
Start Adam from two initialisations
related by a generic rotation,

135
00:10:20.184 --> 00:10:22.112
and the runs drift apart.

136
00:10:22.594 --> 00:10:26.239
Relate them by a signed permutation, and
they stay identical.

137
00:10:26.632 --> 00:10:30.443
The optimizer sees more of where you start
than the image does.

138
00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:38.896
One more symmetry hides in the
hyperparameters.

139
00:10:39.076 --> 00:10:44.340
Rescale a block's starting size and its
learning rate, adjust alpha to match,

140
00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:49.802
and under Adam, with epsilon, decay and
clipping off,

141
00:10:49.995 --> 00:10:52.758
the weights follow exactly the same path.

142
00:10:53.344 --> 00:10:56.044
These rescalings form a torus.

143
00:10:56.594 --> 00:11:02.198
LoRA+, which gives B a learning rate
lambda times larger than A's,

144
00:11:02.248 --> 00:11:04.929
is just a point on that torus.

145
00:11:05.462 --> 00:11:09.558
That's the coincidence from the start, and
our second nut:

146
00:11:09.811 --> 00:11:11.808
from the usual zero start,

147
00:11:11.923 --> 00:11:18.606
LoRA+ and plain LoRA with alpha times
lambda take the same path, exactly.

148
00:11:19.210 --> 00:11:25.678
Schulman and colleagues found this for
LoRA and Adam in 2025, epsilon included.

149
00:11:25.928 --> 00:11:27.756
The torus carries it to SGD,

150
00:11:27.806 --> 00:11:31.813
and to any method whose update is a
product of its blocks.

151
00:11:42.668 --> 00:11:47.279
Some methods add no adapter at all: they
train the weights directly,

152
00:11:47.329 --> 00:11:49.037
but compress the gradient.

153
00:11:49.273 --> 00:11:55.272
GaLore projects each gradient onto a small
subspace and runs Adam there.

154
00:11:55.572 --> 00:12:01.378
Backpropagation has the shape of a lens: a
forward map paired with a backward one.

155
00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:02.798
Through that lens,

156
00:12:02.848 --> 00:12:07.827
a linear adapter and a compressed gradient
are two views of the same thing.

157
00:12:08.296 --> 00:12:13.181
So the third nut: without weight decay or
gradient clipping,

158
00:12:13.317 --> 00:12:18.548
GaLore is exactly one-sided LoRA, with
GaLore's projector as the frozen factor,

159
00:12:18.598 --> 00:12:23.338
merged and restarted every period, its
optimizer state carried over.

160
00:12:23.607 --> 00:12:27.858
Torroba-Hennigen and colleagues proved
this for GaLore in 2025.

161
00:12:28.113 --> 00:12:32.506
The lens gives it a short proof by
induction, for every linear adapter.

162
00:12:43.429 --> 00:12:46.660
So far θ₀ has stayed put.

163
00:12:46.979 --> 00:12:48.859
But plenty of things move it.

164
00:12:49.235 --> 00:12:54.644
Quantise the model to four bits, and the
frozen weights snap to a grid.

165
00:12:55.157 --> 00:13:00.377
QLoRA is ordinary LoRA pointed at the
snapped weights, not the real ones.

166
00:13:00.427 --> 00:13:06.920
So it starts off target by the
quantisation error, which is generically
full rank,

167
00:13:07.246 --> 00:13:12.646
and it can only get back to the original
point if that error has rank at most r.

168
00:13:14.516 --> 00:13:17.369
Merging moves θ₀ too.

169
00:13:17.992 --> 00:13:21.634
Train an adapter, merge it into the
weights,

170
00:13:21.824 --> 00:13:25.471
and start a fresh one from the new point.

171
00:13:25.521 --> 00:13:31.720
Every cycle re-points the method, and the
reach grows: after K cycles,

172
00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:37.476
ReLoRA reaches exactly the updates of rank
at most K times r.

173
00:13:39.133 --> 00:13:41.401
Now the last nut.

174
00:13:41.652 --> 00:13:46.060
A method that rotates the weights from the
input side, exactly,

175
00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:49.749
never changes the inner products between
neurons.

176
00:13:50.105 --> 00:13:52.942
That is what OFT was designed for.

177
00:13:52.992 --> 00:13:56.872
So it never changes a single singular
value,

178
00:13:57.037 --> 00:14:01.592
and in exact arithmetic you can merge as
often as you like:

179
00:14:02.115 --> 00:14:05.641
a rotation times a rotation is still a
rotation.

180
00:14:06.022 --> 00:14:09.524
One caution that matters in practice.

181
00:14:09.991 --> 00:14:20.049
The default OFT in recent versions of
Hugging Face's PEFT library uses an
approximation that isn't exactly
orthogonal.

182
00:14:20.228 --> 00:14:24.417
Each merge shrinks the weights a little,
and in the paper's test,

183
00:14:24.494 --> 00:14:28.962
two hundred merges move the neuron inner
products by about a fifth.

184
00:14:40.929 --> 00:14:44.682
Do this for every method, and you get an
atlas.

185
00:14:44.962 --> 00:14:50.251
The paper reads seven coordinates off each
method's formula: where it acts;

186
00:14:50.588 --> 00:14:57.232
the shape of its image; where it starts,
at a tip, a smooth point, or off target;

187
00:14:57.550 --> 00:15:03.646
its gauge; which changes of basis it
respects; whether it can be merged;

188
00:15:03.784 --> 00:15:06.433
and what merge-and-restart can reach.

189
00:15:06.951 --> 00:15:12.281
Three hundred and eighty-two methods from
a hundred and thirty-eight papers.

190
00:15:12.565 --> 00:15:16.771
Four hundred and three arrows saying which
method simulates which,

191
00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:18.625
each with an explicit map,

192
00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:23.717
and four hundred and eighty-five
obstructions saying which can't.

193
00:15:25.248 --> 00:15:27.960
A theory should also risk being wrong,

194
00:15:28.189 --> 00:15:32.292
so the atlas makes predictions a training
run can test.

195
00:15:32.554 --> 00:15:38.236
Under Adam, a fair LoRA+ baseline should
multiply alpha by lambda.

196
00:15:38.395 --> 00:15:43.638
LoRA's charge should drift by a second-
order amount per step of SGD,

197
00:15:43.688 --> 00:15:47.543
a cheap diagnostic of what your optimizer
adds.

198
00:15:47.949 --> 00:15:50.684
And it marks its own limits.

199
00:15:51.159 --> 00:15:54.310
Reaching a weight isn't the same as
learning it.

200
00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:58.368
Most results are about images, in exact
arithmetic.

201
00:15:58.789 --> 00:16:02.558
And words like gauge and Noether are
analogies;

202
00:16:02.873 --> 00:16:05.777
the paper says exactly where each one
stops.

203
00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:12.006
So the next time a new method appears, you
don't have to reach for the hammer.

204
00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:13.947
Ask three questions.

205
00:16:14.495 --> 00:16:16.069
What can its map reach?

206
00:16:16.644 --> 00:16:19.688
How do its fibres meet the optimizer?

207
00:16:20.024 --> 00:16:22.691
And what happens when the base point
moves?

208
00:16:23.470 --> 00:16:26.519
Often, the answer is already under the
water.

209
00:16:27.521 --> 00:16:29.979
The proofs, the full catalogue,

210
00:16:30.029 --> 00:16:35.096
and twenty interactive figures are in the
paper and on the companion site.

211
00:16:35.146 --> 00:16:36.624
The link is below.

212
00:16:36.993 --> 00:16:38.132
Thanks for watching.
